			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4331965">
									

<p>Note the second experiment:</p>
<p>&quot;Subject viewed the image for 2 minutes before experiencing hemorrhaging of the tear ducts. Subject was incapacitated by a severe headache for 4.5 hours. Upon recovery, subject described the experience as &quot;like [his] head was trying to pop.&quot; Subject survived, with moderate vision loss.&quot;</p>
<p>I feel the article establishes fairly clearly that the kid is recreating the events of the Incident with his drawings. Not too big a stretch to say a child experiences an infohazard as extreme pressure in his head and has the (apparently correct) idea of relieving the pressure.</p>
<p>Overall, this skip is heartwarming and a perfect example of the Ethics Committee doing its job correctly, even if I do think it works better as an example of that in the absence of the last tab. Giving him the blanket has enough impact as an ending.</p>

